# Git commit reminders

Do **not** auto-commit. Still only run `git commit` after the owner explicitly says yes / "commit".

**Do** proactively remind them to commit when uncommitted work exists and any of these is true:

- A meaningful chunk of work just finished (feature, fix, refactor, docs batch)
- Roughly **30+ minutes** of editing in the same chat, or after several substantial change rounds
- Many files changed, or a large diff (especially `favorites.js` / helper / manifest)
- About to do a risky step (reload extension, deploy helper, reset, checkout, pull, large rewrite)

Reminder style: one short question, e.g.  
`Uncommitted changes are sitting only in the working tree — commit now?`

Do not nag every message. If they decline, wait until the next milestone above before asking again.

When they agree to commit, follow the normal commit protocol (status/diff/log, HEREDOC message, no force/hooks skip, no push unless asked).
